WEBVTT
TITLE Bitwise Operators and Bit Manipulation in C: Masks, Fields, Shifts and the Four Cases With No Answer — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Two bytes, one above the other. A bitwise operator works straight down the columns, like eight tiny sums done at once.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:14.000
AND keeps a one only where both columns hold a one. Here that leaves one column out of three.

3
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:21.000
OR keeps a one where either holds one. XOR keeps a one where they disagree. Three tables, same eight columns.

4
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:28.000
This is why & is not &&. Logical and gives 0 or 1; bitwise and gives a number with bits in it.

5
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:35.000
AND as a table: four rows, one output. Zero wins everywhere except the single row where both inputs are one.

6
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:42.000
OR is one unless both are zero. XOR is one when the inputs differ, which is why it is the parity operator.

7
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:49.000
Sixteen binary Boolean functions exist. C gives you three as operators, and one unary: the complement.

8
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:56.000
Because the columns are independent, the Boolean laws hold bitwise: De Morgan works on every bit at once.

9
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:03.000
A mask is a number with exactly one one in it. Shift one left by k and you have a mask for bit k.

10
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:10.000
OR sets the bit. AND with the complement clears it. XOR toggles it. AND with the mask reads it.

11
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:17.000
On 182 the mask for bit three is absent and the mask for bit two is present. Watch the same number change and come back.

12
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:24.000
The test is (x >> k) & 1, or equivalently x & mask compared with zero. Both are one operation more than they look.

13
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:31.000
A field is a run of bits in the middle of a number. To read bits five down to two, shift right by two first.

14
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:38.000
Then mask with four ones. Shift first, mask second, and the field is a number you can compare.

15
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:45.000
Mask first and the field is already gone: the shift then throws away the bits you kept. The order is not a style choice.

16
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.000
A mask of w ones is (1 << w) - 1, and w equal to the width is the shift that has no answer.

17
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:59.000
Five is 00000101. Complement every bit and you get 11111010, which is not a number you would guess.

18
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:06.000
The rule is ~x = -x - 1, for every signed value. Complement is not negation, and the two differ by one.

19
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:13.000
A negative operand works too: its pattern is used, and the sign bit is all ones. So mask after, not before.

20
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Widening a negative value copies the sign bit all the way up. Widening an unsigned one fills with zeros instead.

21
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:27.000
Shifting left by k multiplies by two to the k. Shifting right divides, dropping the remainder.

22
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:34.000
The count must be at least zero and less than the width. A count equal to the width is undefined, not zero.

23
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:41.000
Shifting a negative value left is undefined, and so is shifting a positive one into the sign bit. Use unsigned.

24
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:48.000
Right shifting a negative value is implementation-defined: arithmetic gives minus four, logical gives one hundred and twenty-four.

25
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:55.000
Subtract one from a number and every bit below its lowest set bit flips to one; that bit itself becomes zero.

26
00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:02.000
So x & (x-1) deletes exactly that bit and keeps everything above it. Repeat and the number drains one one at a time.

27
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:09.000
That gives a power-of-two test in one line: a positive x is a power of two exactly when x & (x-1) is zero.

28
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:16.000
One value passes the test and is not a power of two: zero. So the test is written x && !(x & (x-1)).

29
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:23.000
Negating a pattern means complementing it and adding one. Do that to 182 and you get 74, whose low bits are the ones you want.

30
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:30.000
x & -x keeps only the lowest set bit: here two. Everything above it cancels, because the negation has zeros there.

31
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.000
Counting is the same trick in a loop: clear the lowest one each time, and the loop runs once per one.

32
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:44.000
Parity is the same information folded down: three XORs reduce a byte to one bit, which is the odd-or-even answer.

33
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:51.000
In C the comparison operators bind tighter than the bitwise ones. So == is applied before &.

34
00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:58.000
So x & 1 == 0 means x & (1 == 0). One equals zero is false, which is zero, and anything anded with zero is zero.

35
00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:05.000
For x equal to five the intended test answers one and the written test answers zero. The two disagree on every even number.

36
00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:12.000
The repair is parentheses, not a different operator. Put them round the bit work and the ladder stops mattering.

37
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:19.000
XOR is its own inverse. That is enough to exchange two values with no third variable.

38
00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:26.000
Five and nine: after three XORs the first holds nine and the second holds five. The values have exchanged places.

39
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:33.000
But if both names refer to the same object, the first XOR zeroes it, and the other two keep it zero. The value is gone.

40
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:40.000
A compiler already swaps through a register. The trick is worth knowing for the reading, not for the writing.

41
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:47.000
GATE 2022, Question 44: two characters, three bitwise operators, printed with the character conversion.

42
00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:54.000
Eighty and one twenty give eighty; plus forty-two is one twenty-two, the letter z. The other two follow the same way.

43
00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:01.000
GATE 2017 Set 1, Question 55: a loop that adds the low bit and shifts. That is a count of ones, one bit at a time.

44
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:08.000
The counter is static, so it accumulates. The calls return 2, 3, 5, 6, 7 and the total printed is 23.

45
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:15.000
Step one: name the width and the signedness. Every mask and every shift count depends on both.

46
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:22.000
Step two: write masks as 1u << k or (1u << w) - 1, in an unsigned type. Never shift by the width.

47
00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:29.000
Step three: fields are shift-then-mask, counting is x & (x-1), a bit test is (x >> k) & 1 with parentheses.

48
00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:36.000
Step four: check the sign and the count. Step five: verify on a byte by hand. If it is undefined, say so and stop.

